# Get an ore within mining reach. It is a skill of its own so that a failed
# search is ONE failure of the caller's rule, like find_stone: the caller then
# falls through to its next rule. Ore sits inside rock and no ray from the
# surface sees it, so the surface search (search_surface) comes first and, when
# it finds nothing, `branch_mine` opens the rock: a stair down to the ore's best
# height, then a tunnel in legs that turn.
#
# Why search, then branch_mine, without the two rules fighting (interpreter
# 6.1): the highest true rule fires, but a rule whose child just failed is
# ineligible for that one tick only. So the search comes first and
# `branch_mine` fires exactly on the tick the search frame failed (three failed
# explores inside search_surface, one cap). It is a discrete primitive, which
# the interpreter runs to its outcome before it evaluates any guard again, so
# the search cannot take over mid-tunnel. It confirms once the ore is known
# within mining reach, and the guard on top then ends the skill. The failure
# cap counts per rule (the third failure of one rule fails the skill, normally
# not_found): a failed search and a failed tunnel alternate until the cap, and
# a finished rule clears its own count.
#
# The skill fails with the last failure kind: normally not_found from the
# tunnel, but a tunnel that keeps refusing (e.g. fluid) can reach its cap
# first, and then the skill fails with that kind instead. A caller must not
# read every failure as "keep searching".
skill find_ore(ore: block)
  doc "Get the given ore within mining reach: search the surface, then tunnel underground; fails with the last failure kind (normally not_found) when it is not found."
  rules:
    near($ore, 5) -> done
    not near($ore, 5) -> do search_surface($ore)
    not near($ore, 5) -> branch_mine($ore)
